{"hash":"418cb82ee7a82276","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":82.085,"said":"Jai Somnath! Come, walk with me. Do you feel the salt wind? We are at Prabhas Patan, at the very edge of India, where the land ends and the Arabian Sea begins. You have just passed through the Digvijay Dwar, the great gateway, named for Jam Saheb Digvijaysinhji of Nawanagar, who led the trust that rebuilt this temple and made its first donation. Now look at the bronze figure standing guard. That is Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel, and our story begins with him. Picture this shore in November 1947. India has been free for only a few months. Sardar Patel walks here among broken stones and fallen pillars, the ruins of a temple that has been destroyed and raised again many times over the centuries. That same evening he stands before a crowd and promises that Somnath will rise once more. Ordinary devotees give what they can, and the promise is kept. But why did this one temple matter so much? To understand, we must first go down to the sea, where the story of Somnath truly begins.\n\nDid you know? Sardar Patel died on 15 December 1950, five months before the consecration he had set in motion; his statue now greets every pilgrim at the gate.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":983},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":999,"end":1144}],"words":[[0.098,0.325,0,3],[0.435,0.685,4,11],[2.055,0.433,13,17],[2.656,0.312,19,23],[2.981,0.204,24,28],[3.197,0.288,29,31],[4.351,0.168,33,35],[4.531,0.108,36,39],[4.651,0.252,40,44],[4.916,0.084,45,48],[5.012,0.349,49,53],[5.373,0.409,54,58],[6.623,0.228,60,62],[6.863,0.156,63,66],[7.031,0.132,67,69],[7.176,0.493,70,77],[7.68,0.553,78,83],[8.401,0.12,85,87],[8.534,0.096,88,91],[8.642,0.385,92,96],[9.038,0.156,97,101],[9.207,0.144,102,104],[9.363,0.469,105,110],[10.084,0.192,112,117],[10.288,0.084,118,121],[10.385,0.433,122,126],[10.829,0.3,127,131],[11.142,0.132,132,135],[11.286,0.084,136,139],[11.382,0.493,140,147],[11.887,0.216,148,151],[12.115,0